<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Weijie J. Su
</span><span id="id6" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>University of Pennsylvania; Email: <span id="id6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">suw@wharton.upenn.edu</span>. </span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">June 27, 2025</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Text watermarks in large language models (LLMs) are an increasingly important tool for detecting synthetic text and distinguishing human-written content from LLM-generated text. While most existing studies focus on determining whether entire texts are watermarked, many real-world scenarios involve mixed-source texts, which blend human-written and watermarked content. In this paper, we address the problem of optimally estimating the watermark proportion in mixed-source texts. We cast this problem as estimating the proportion parameter in a mixture model based on <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pivotal statistics</em>. First, we show that this parameter is not even identifiable in certain watermarking schemes, let alone consistently estimable. In stark contrast, for watermarking methods that employ continuous pivotal statistics for detection, we demonstrate that the proportion parameter is identifiable under mild conditions. We propose efficient estimators for this class of methods, which include several popular unbiased watermarks as examples, and derive minimax lower bounds for any measurable estimator based on pivotal statistics, showing that our estimators achieve these lower bounds. Through evaluations on both synthetic data and mixed-source text generated by open-source models, we demonstrate that our proposed estimators consistently achieve high estimation accuracy.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h3>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Over the past two years, the rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) has profoundly transformed content generation, with diverse applications spanning creative writing, code generation, text summarization, and educational assistance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. Their rapid adoption, however, has raised pressing concerns regarding content authenticity in critical domains such as scientific peer review <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, education <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, journalism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, and data authenticity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate potential misuse of these powerful generative tools, researchers have developed <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">text watermarking</em> methods that embed imperceptible yet statistically verifiable signals into LLM-generated output <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. These watermarking techniques facilitate reliable detection of synthetic text, enabling one to distinguish fully watermarked content from human-written text <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, real-world scenarios frequently involve mixed-source texts, where human-written and LLM-generated content are commonly interwoven <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. This is particularly evident in academic peer reviews, as demonstrated by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al. [21]</a></cite>, who reported a growing prevalence of LLM-modified reviews in machine learning conferences. Beyond academia, evidence suggests that human-AI collaboration is increasingly reshaping content creation practices across various domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. In this context, quantifying the extent of human versus LLM contribution becomes crucial <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. Indeed, accurate estimates of human-written proportions have significant implications for determining content ownership and authenticity in academic writing, education, and legal documents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. For instance, a homework assignment essay with only 10% student contribution might be deemed unacceptable, while one with 80% or more student input might be considered permissible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While prior studies have primarily focused on binary detection of fully watermarked versus fully human-written texts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, recent work has begun to address the challenges posed by mixed-source content. From a statistical standpoint, however, the problem of estimating the proportion of LLM-generated content in mixed-source texts remains insufficiently explored, despite its critical role in use cases such as authorship attribution. Notably, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [19]</a></cite> formulated watermark detection in mixed-source texts as a mixture detection problem and identified optimal detection methods through the lens of statistical robustness. Other studies have examined the more granular problem of identifying watermarked segments within mixed-source texts. Examples include <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aaronson [1]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirchenbauer et al. [16]</a></cite>’s continuous span search, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. [44]</a></cite>’s online learning approach, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [18]</a></cite>’s application of change point detection to identify watermarked segments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">While identifying watermarked segments can, in principle, suffice for our purpose, it often yields an unnecessarily fine level of granularity and, more critically, may be statistically infeasible for pinpointing precisely which segments are human-authored. In many scenarios, a single numerical estimate of the proportion of LLM-generated content is more convenient and interpretable. For instance, it is practical to require AI-generated content to not exceed a 10% threshold in critical documents such as legal briefs and academic papers, while a higher limit (e.g., 50%) may be tolerable in less critical contexts such as homework assignments. Moreover, precisely localizing human-written text is arguably more challenging than simply estimating the overall proportion. This parallels findings from the broader mixture detection literature, wherein identifying individual signal instances is inherently more difficult than detecting their aggregate presence. Indeed, signals can be statistically detectable even when it is information-theoretically impossible to locate each instance accurately <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Our Contributions</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we address the problem of estimating the proportion of tokens—smallest units in LLM generation that, without loss of generality, can be thought of as words—generated by watermarked LLMs in mixed-source texts under general watermarking schemes. Our main contributions are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem formulation and identifiability</span>: We formulate the estimation problem as a mixture model parameter estimation task using pivotal statistics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.
Roughly speaking, a pivotal statistic quantifies the evidence that a given word (or token) is watermarked. Through rigorous analysis, we show that the proportion parameter in green-red list watermarking schemes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> is not identifiable, which leads to the inherent bias in traditional approaches like maximum likelihood estimation (MLE). However, for watermarks with continuous pivotal statistics, we establish conditions under which identifiability can be achieved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">New estimators with theoretical guarantees</span>: We develop computationally efficient estimators for identifiable cases and provide theoretical guarantees for their estimation accuracy. Our comprehensive experimental validation, conducted on both synthetic data and mixed-source texts from open-source LLMs, shows the consistent and superior performance of our proposed estimators.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Matched minimax lower bounds</span>: We establish minimax lower bounds for broad classes of measurable estimators and prove that our proposed methods generally achieve these bounds, thereby establishing their theoretical optimality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Related Work</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Most watermarking studies focus on the binary detection problem, which aims to determine whether a text is fully watermarked or entirely human-written. However, this narrow scope fails to address real-world scenarios where texts often originate from mixed sources. While recent work has begun exploring the detection of watermarked segments in mixed-source texts, their approaches differ significantly from ours.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [19]</a></cite> investigated the optimal detection of watermarked segments under human editing, assuming that the text is initially fully generated by a watermarked LLM and later modified by a human editor. Using the Gumbel-max watermark as an example, they demonstrate that a family of truncated goodness-of-fit tests achieves optimal detection, where optimality is defined by the phase transition boundary and feast-favorable efficiency notion introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. However, their work focuses exclusively on detection and does not address estimation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Other researchers attempt to locate watermarked segments by assigning scores to individual tokens or intervals of tokens and identifying watermarked (or LLM-generated) content based on extreme values. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirchenbauer et al. [16]</a></cite> searched for the largest intervals or continuous token spans with the highest <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-scores by testing all possible window sizes and traversing the entire text for each size, resulting in a computational complexity of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aaronson [1]</a></cite> improved on this by using dynamic programming to identify the largest continuous token span, achieving a reduced complexity of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{1.5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>1.5</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{1.5})</annotation></semantics></math>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. [44]</a></cite> defined token scores based on the expectations of pivotal statistics and employs an online learning algorithm for estimation, achieving a complexity of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n\log n)</annotation></semantics></math> for texts of length <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Inspired by change point detection, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [18]</a></cite> used bootstrap-based <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values as token scores, but the method incurs a higher computational cost of approximately <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> due to its analysis of dependencies across all sliding token windows and pseudorandomness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these advancements, existing methods neither address the problem of watermark proportion estimation nor provide a systematic analysis of their performance or hyperparameter tuning (e.g., threshold selection). In contrast, our work presents the first comprehensive framework for watermark proportion estimation. We address key questions such as what to estimate (estimand and estimability), how to estimate (estimation methods), how accurately it can be estimated (estimation accuracy), and the theoretical limits of estimation (minimax lower bounds). Furthermore, our proposed estimator achieves <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{O}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{O}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> complexity by requiring only a logarithmic number of passes through the samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h3>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs, such as the GPT series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, generate text by autoregressively sampling discrete units called tokens. Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathcal{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathcal{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the vocabulary (token universe), and let <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}\in{{\mathcal{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}\in{{\mathcal{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a single token. At each step <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, given the prefix <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{w}_{1:(t-1)}:={w}_{1}{w}_{2}\cdots{w}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{1:(t-1)}:={w}_{1}{w}_{2}\cdots{w}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model computes a multinomial distribution <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}:=(P_{t,w})_{w\in{{\mathcal{W}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}:=(P_{t,w})_{w\in{{\mathcal{W}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> over the vocabulary, and samples the next token <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}\sim\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}\sim\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This distribution <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, called the next-token prediction (NTP) distribution, is unknown to the verifier, as it depends on the user-supplied prompt, and potentially hidden system prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">A general framework for watermarking.</h6>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We adopt a general three-party framework that reflects real-world applications of watermark-based verification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.
The language model provider (e.g., OpenAI) embeds statistical watermark signals into the generated text using a designated watermarking scheme. Under this setup, every token generated by the model is watermarked by design.
The user (e.g., a student) interacts with the model, receives the watermarked output, and may optionally modify the text before sharing or submitting it.
The verifier (e.g., a teacher) is tasked with estimating how many tokens in a given document originated from the watermarked LLM.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Watermarking schemes operate by modifying the standard sampling process to embed statistical signals that can later be recovered for verification. This is typically achieved by introducing a pseudorandom variable <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ζ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at each step <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, which serves as a random seed that guides token selection. Instead of sampling <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> directly from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model applies a decoding rule <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}:={\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P}_{t},\zeta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">:=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ζ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}:={\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P}_{t},\zeta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a (possibly stochastic) decoding function. The pseudorandom variable <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ζ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated using cryptographic techniques and can be deterministically reconstructed by the verifier if the construction is known.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1</span></span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For example, </span><math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\zeta_{t}={\mathcal{A}}({w}_{(t-m):(t-1)},{\mathtt{Key}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ζ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝙺𝚎𝚢</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}={\mathcal{A}}({w}_{(t-m):(t-1)},{\mathtt{Key}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> for some hash function </span><math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, context window size </span><math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and secret key </span><math id="footnote1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathtt{Key}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝙺𝚎𝚢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathtt{Key}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Given the full text </span><math id="footnote1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{w}_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the hash function, and the key, one can reconstruct </span><math id="footnote1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\zeta_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ζ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></span></span></span> In this work, we assume <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ζ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are i.i.d. samples from a known distribution, consistent with the perfect pseudorandomness assumption used in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>. A watermark is called unbiased if the resulting token still marginally follows the original NTP distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, it is biased <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In contrast, human-written tokens are also drawn from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> but do not depend on <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ζ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to a different joint distribution over <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ζ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Detection thus reduces to testing whether <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ζ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are statistically dependent. For human-written text, they are independent, since human users know nothing about the pseudorandomness; for watermarked text, dependence is introduced through the decoding rule <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
To detect this dependence, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [20]</a></cite> proposed computing a pivotal statistic <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=Y({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=Y({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, designed to control Type I error and boost power.
These statistics are scalars that summarize the degree of dependence between <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which follow a known distribution under the null (i.e., no watermark). Specifically, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}\sim\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}\sim\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. when no watermark is present, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}\mid\bm{P}_{t}\sim\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}\mid\bm{P}_{t}\sim\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> under the alternative due to watermark signals.
Many watermarking schemes can be cast in this form, and detection is based solely on the sequence <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math>. This approach enables the verifier to statistically infer the presence and extent of watermarked content, even without access to the original prompt or NTP distributions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S2.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Summary of three watermarks. </span></figcaption>
<div id="S2.T1.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:80.3pt;vertical-align:-38.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-184.2pt,42.9pt) scale(0.483542929225746,0.483542929225746) ;">
<table id="S2.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S2.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S2.T1.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Watermark</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S2.T1.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pseudorandom <math id="S2.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S2.T1.5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decoder <math id="S2.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P},\zeta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P},\zeta)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:91.0pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.T1.5.1.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pivotal statistic</span></span>
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.1.4.1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y({w},\zeta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y({w},\zeta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><math id="S2.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="S2.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1,\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑷</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1,\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Gumbel-max</span></span>
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.1.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.1.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:91.0pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta=(U_{1},\ldots,U_{|{{\mathcal{W}}}|})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta=(U_{1},\ldots,U_{|{{\mathcal{W}}}|})</annotation></semantics></math> with each <math id="S2.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. from <math id="S2.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:88.2pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Gumbel-max trick:</span>
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.3.1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arg\max_{{w}}\frac{\log\zeta_{{w}}}{P_{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arg\max_{{w}}\frac{\log\zeta_{{w}}}{P_{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> (which follows <math id="S2.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:79.7pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{w}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:91.0pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">The CDF is: <math id="S2.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\limits_{{w}}P_{{w}}r^{1/P_{{w}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>w</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\limits_{{w}}P_{{w}}r^{1/P_{{w}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.5.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Inverse transform</span></span>
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.1.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.1.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:91.0pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta=(U,\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta=(U,\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\sim{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\sim{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> a permutation on <math id="S2.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathcal{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathcal{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:88.2pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}\circ F_{\pi}^{-1}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}\circ F_{\pi}^{-1}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\pi}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\pi}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the generalized inverse of <math id="S2.T1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:88.2pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.T1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-|U-\eta_{\pi}({w})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-|U-\eta_{\pi}({w})|</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.T1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\pi}({w})=\frac{\pi({w})-1}{|{{\mathcal{W}}}|-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\pi}({w})=\frac{\pi({w})-1}{|{{\mathcal{W}}}|-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:79.7pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.5.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Asymp. CDF: <math id="S2.T1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.T1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:91.0pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.3.6.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Asymp. CDF is <math id="S2.T1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\frac{1-r}{P_{(1)}})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\frac{1-r}{P_{(1)}})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.T1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in[1-P_{(1)},1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in[1-P_{(1)},1]</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.5.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Green-red list</span></span>
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:91.0pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.T1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta={\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta={\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a random set of <math id="S2.T1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathcal{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathcal{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.T1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{G}}|=|{{\mathcal{W}}}|\cdot\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{G}}|=|{{\mathcal{W}}}|\cdot\gamma</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:88.2pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">sampled from <math id="S2.T1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{gr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{gr}</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.T1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{gr})_{{w}}\propto P_{w}\cdot\exp(\delta{\bf 1}_{{w}\in{\mathcal{G}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{gr})_{{w}}\propto P_{w}\cdot\exp(\delta{\bf 1}_{{w}\in{\mathcal{G}}})</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:88.2pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.T1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf 1}_{{w}\in{\mathcal{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf 1}_{{w}\in{\mathcal{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, whether the token <math id="S2.T1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is in the green set <math id="S2.T1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:79.7pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.5.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.T1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Ber(\gamma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ber</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Ber(\gamma)}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.5.1.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:91.0pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.5.1.4.6.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.T1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Ber(\mu)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ber</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Ber(\mu)}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.T1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=\frac{\sum\limits_{{w}\in{\mathcal{G}}}P_{{w}}\exp(\delta)}{\sum\limits_{{w}\in{\mathcal{G}}}P_{{w}}\exp(\delta)+\sum\limits_{{w}\notin{\mathcal{G}}}P_{{w}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><munder><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><munder><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><munder><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\frac{\sum\limits_{{w}\in{\mathcal{G}}}P_{{w}}\exp(\delta)}{\sum\limits_{{w}\in{\mathcal{G}}}P_{{w}}\exp(\delta)+\sum\limits_{{w}\notin{\mathcal{G}}}P_{{w}}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite></span>
</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Three representative watermarks.</h6>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We study three representative watermarking schemes under the general decoding framework described above. These schemes differ primarily in the choice of decoder <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the construction of the pseudorandom number <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ζ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Once these components are specified, the watermarking scheme is fully determined. For detection purposes, once the pivotal statistics are defined, the null distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the alternative distributions <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1,\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1,\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> are also fixed. Therefore, it suffices to highlight these structural differences. Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ A general framework for watermarking. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the key components of each scheme.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We briefly introduce each scheme below.
The Gumbel-max watermark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, recognized as the first unbiased watermark, has been implemented internally at OpenAI. It uses the Gumbel-max trick <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> to sample tokens, which by construction ensures unbiasedness.
The inverse transform watermark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> applies inverse transform sampling to the CDF <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\pi}(x)=\sum_{{w}}P_{{w}}{\bf 1}_{\pi({w})\leq x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∑</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝟏</mn><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≤</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\pi}(x)=\sum_{{w}}P_{{w}}{\bf 1}_{\pi({w})\leq x}</annotation></semantics></math>, which defines a distribution over the vocabulary <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathcal{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathcal{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math> based on a permutation <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.
The green-red list watermark, though biased, is widely used due to its simplicity. It partitions the vocabulary into green and red token sets, secretly boosts the probability of green tokens, and signals watermarking if the observed frequency of green tokens exceeds an expected threshold. Follow-up work has improved this scheme through parameter tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> and by developing unbiased <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> or robust variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Proportion Estimation Methods</h3>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we study how to estimate the proportion of watermarked content in mixed-source text. Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> defines the problem, Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> analyzes its estimability, and Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Estimation Methods ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> presents our estimators.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Target Estimand</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first step is to identify the quantity we aim to estimate and the specific model where it arises.
Most existing watermark detection methods rely on pivotal statistics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, which provide quantitative evidence of watermark signals at each token–pseudorandom pair <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Those statistics exhibit a key dichotomy based on watermark integrity.
When the watermark signal in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is disrupted—such as by modifying <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> or altering its preceding tokens so that <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> no longer generates <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>—<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes statistically independent of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> due to the statistical sensitivity of hash functions.
In such cases, the pivotal function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=Y({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=Y({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> always follows a known distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, regardless of the distribution of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, if the watermark signal in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({w}_{t},\zeta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> remains intact, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> continues to depend on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> via the decoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}\sim\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}\sim\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the NTP distribution for <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
So, given <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> follows either the null <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> or the alternative <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The expected proportion following the alternative distributions naturally becomes our target estimand, as in Model <a href="#Thmmodel1" title="Model 1 ( [ ] ). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmodel1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_model">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmodel1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model 1</span></span><span id="Thmmodel1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [19]</a></cite>)<span id="Thmmodel1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmodel1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmodel1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmodel1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The pivotal statistic <math id="Thmmodel1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y_{t}|\bm{P}_{t}\sim(1-{\varepsilon})\mu_{0}+{\varepsilon}\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}},\quad\forall t\in[n]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}|\bm{P}_{t}\sim(1-{\varepsilon})\mu_{0}+{\varepsilon}\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}},\quad\forall t\in[n],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmmodel1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmodel1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmmodel1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the proportion of watermark signals, our estimand of interest. Rigorously, there exists a <math id="Thmmodel1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-field <math id="Thmmodel1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\mathcal{F}}_{t}\}_{t\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\mathcal{F}}_{t}\}_{t\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="Thmmodel1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmmodel1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\EB[\mathbf{1}\{Y_{t}\leq x\}|{\mathcal{F}}_{t-1}]=(1-{\varepsilon})F_{0}(x)+{\varepsilon}F_{\bm{P}_{t}}(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB[\mathbf{1}\{Y_{t}\leq x\}|{\mathcal{F}}_{t-1}]=(1-{\varepsilon})F_{0}(x)+{\varepsilon}F_{\bm{P}_{t}}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmmodel1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmodel1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here, <math id="Thmmodel1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the history of tokens <math id="Thmmodel1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{w}_{1:(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{1:(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we view it as <math id="Thmmodel1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>-measurable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-field <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℱ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the full information available up to token <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, including the generation history <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{w}_{1:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{1:t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the NTP distributions <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{P}_{1:(t+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐏</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{1:(t+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The formulation in (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Model 1 ( [ ] ). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) implies that, conditioned on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐏</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the pivotal statistic <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Y</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> follows a mixture of the null distribution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the alternative <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐏</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1,\bm{P}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>. While <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℱ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a useful concept, it includes unobservable components like <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐏</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which the verifier does not have access to in practice. This model thus reflects the verifier’s uncertainty over user edits without assuming any specific editing strategy.<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">While Model <a href="#Thmmodel1" title="Model 1 ( [ ] ). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> accurately captures the autoregressive behavior of LLMs, its application in practice and theory is constrained by two factors. First, from a practical standpoint, the verifier does not have access to critical components of the filtration <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℱ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, particularly each NTP distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which depend on the original prompt and the internal state of the LLM. This makes it infeasible to use the exact filtration in any detection or estimation procedure. Second, from a theoretical standpoint, the fact that each <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> evolves with the token history introduces complex temporal dependencies. Analyzing this dependence typically requires advanced martingale-based techniques, as seen in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. These challenges motivate the use of a simplified surrogate model, which we describe next.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmodel2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_model">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmodel2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model 2</span></span><span id="Thmmodel2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Surrogate model)<span id="Thmmodel2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmodel2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmodel2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmodel2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We assume that each <math id="Thmmodel2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed at the start of a generation, and the pivotal statistic <math id="Thmmodel2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is still generated according to (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Model 1 ( [ ] ). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). In this setup, <math id="Thmmodel2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent but not identically distributed.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The key difference between Model <a href="#Thmmodel2" title="Model 2 (Surrogate model). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Model <a href="#Thmmodel1" title="Model 1 ( [ ] ). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is the removal of the statistical dependence of the NTP distributions on token history. <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
We argue that Model 2 is a realistic and reasonable simplification—primarily because, from the verifier’s perspective, Models 1 and 2 are operationally indistinguishable.
This indistinguishability arises from the fact that the verifier lacks access to critical internal information used during generation, such as the original prompt, the internal state of the language model, and the NTP distributions themselves.
This can be further illustrated through the following thought experiment.
Consider a sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math> generated under Model 1, where each <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the full token history: <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}={\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P}_{t},\zeta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ζ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}={\mathcal{S}}(\bm{P}_{t},\zeta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}=\mathrm{LLM}(w_{1:(t-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">LLM</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}=\mathrm{LLM}(w_{1:(t-1)})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now imagine freezing this exact sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{P}_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math> and defining a new model, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LLM}_{\mathrm{new}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">LLM</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">new</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LLM}_{\mathrm{new}}</annotation></semantics></math>, that always uses the pre-computed <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at step <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to sample tokens regardless of the generation history.
If <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math> is produced by <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LLM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">LLM</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LLM}</annotation></semantics></math>, we are in Model 1; if by <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LLM}_{\mathrm{new}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">LLM</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">new</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LLM}_{\mathrm{new}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we are in Model 2.
Although the internal mechanisms differ, both models yield the same tokens and pivotal statistics—making them indistinguishable to the verifier, who observes only the final tokens and pseudorandom numbers, but not the model internals.
For this reason, we adopt Model <a href="#Thmmodel2" title="Model 2 (Surrogate model). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for method development and theoretical analysis, while evaluating our methods under the more realistic Model <a href="#Thmmodel1" title="Model 1 ( [ ] ). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in experiments.
As we will show, the estimators derived under Model <a href="#Thmmodel2" title="Model 2 (Surrogate model). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> remain robust and effective when applied to data generated under Model <a href="#Thmmodel1" title="Model 1 ( [ ] ). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is worth noting that the watermark proportion <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined in terms of pivotal statistics rather than individual tokens. As introduced in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, each pivotal statistic is a deterministic function of consecutive tokens. As a result, modifying a single token may affect multiple pivotal statistics, leading to a discrepancy between the proportion of watermarked tokens and that of watermarked pivotal statistics. We adopt this definition for its simplicity and because nearly all existing detection rules rely on pivotal statistics.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Estimability</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The next question is whether the proportion <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined—or, more precisely, identifiable—when the NTP distributions <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{P}_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math> are unknown and potentially vary, as is the case for real-world verifiers. Identifiability, in this context, means that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> can be uniquely determined from the observed pivotal statistics alone, even when <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{P}_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math> are unknown and arbitrary.</span>
If <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is not identifiable, multiple values of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> could yield the same data distribution, making it impossible to obtain a unique and accurate estimate. This ambiguity challenges the interpretability of any downstream use of the estimate, as its real-world meaning is unclear.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Impossibility for the green-red list watermark.</h6>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unfortunately, for the green-red list watermark, the proportion <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is not identifiable. To illustrate this, consider a straightforward example of proportion estimation in an i.i.d. binary mixture model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmlem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmlem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmlem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmlem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmlem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmlem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If data points are i.i.d. from the binary mixture <math id="S3.Thmlem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-{\varepsilon})\mathrm{Ber}(\gamma)+{\varepsilon}\mathrm{Ber}(\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Ber</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Ber</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-{\varepsilon})\mathrm{Ber}(\gamma)+{\varepsilon}\mathrm{Ber}(\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> where both <math id="S3.Thmlem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmlem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> are unknown while <math id="S3.Thmlem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is known, then <math id="S3.Thmlem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is not identifiable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The real-world use of green-red list watermarks is more complex than the simplified setting in Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmlem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ Impossibility for the green-red list watermark. ‣ 3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. In practice, each <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}\mid\bm{P}_{t}\sim(1-{\varepsilon})\mathrm{Ber}(\gamma)+{\varepsilon}\mathrm{Ber}(\mu_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Ber</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Ber</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}\mid\bm{P}_{t}\sim(1-{\varepsilon})\mathrm{Ber}(\gamma)+{\varepsilon}\mathrm{Ber}(\mu_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the unknown NTP distribution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The expression for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is given in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ A general framework for watermarking. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. As a result, we arrive at the following corollary. The non-identifiability in this case arises because each <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is binary and thus carries limited information about the underlying parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcor1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcor1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcor1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcor1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcor1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcor1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Model <a href="#Thmmodel2" title="Model 2 (Surrogate model). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-identifiable for the green-red list watermark.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Identifiability for continuous pivotal statistics.</h6>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Interestingly, the estimation problem becomes fundamentally different when <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous. Specifically, by assuming <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1,\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑷</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1,\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> differs uniformly from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmasmp1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ Identifiability for continuous pivotal statistics. ‣ 3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes identifiable (Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmlem2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ Identifiability for continuous pivotal statistics. ‣ 3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmasmp1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_asmp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmasmp1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmasmp1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bm{P}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐏</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bm{P}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the CDF of <math id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1,\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐏</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1,\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, i.e., <math id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}(x)=\mu_{0}(Y\leq x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}(x)=\mu_{0}(Y\leq x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bm{P}}(x)=\mu_{1,\bm{P}}(Y\leq x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐏</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐏</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bm{P}}(x)=\mu_{1,\bm{P}}(Y\leq x)</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that (i) <math id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝐏</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> are differentiable everywhere, (ii) <math id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim\limits_{x\to 0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}F_{\bm{P}}(x)/F_{0}(x)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐏</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim\limits_{x\to 0}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}F_{\bm{P}}(x)/F_{0}(x)}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for any non-singular <math id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is not an entry of <math id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}</annotation></semantics></math>), and (iii) all of NTP distributions <math id="S3.Thmasmp1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{1},\ldots,\bm{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{1},\ldots,\bm{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are non-singular.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmlem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmlem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmlem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmlem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmlem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmlem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmasmp1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ Identifiability for continuous pivotal statistics. ‣ 3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and Model <a href="#Thmmodel2" title="Model 2 (Surrogate model). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <math id="S3.Thmlem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is identifiable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This result applies to both the Gumbel-max and inverse transform watermark schemes, as their pivotal statistics are continuous and satisfy Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmasmp1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ Identifiability for continuous pivotal statistics. ‣ 3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. As a result, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is identifiable for these two watermarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmlem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmlem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmlem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmlem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmlem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmlem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmasmp1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ Identifiability for continuous pivotal statistics. ‣ 3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> holds for the Gumbel-max and inverse transform watermark.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Due to the intricate permutation, the inverse transform watermark satisfies Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmasmp1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ Identifiability for continuous pivotal statistics. ‣ 3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> asymptotically, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{(2)}\cdot\log|{{\mathcal{W}}}|\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{(2)}\cdot\log|{{\mathcal{W}}}|\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{{\mathcal{W}}}|\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{{\mathcal{W}}}|\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. This condition holds in our simulation study, while it may not be satisfied in practice.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Estimation Methods</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proportion <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the equation</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}(x)=(1-{\varepsilon})F_{0}(x)+{\varepsilon}\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)~\text{for any}~x," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>for any</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}(x)=(1-{\varepsilon})F_{0}(x)+{\varepsilon}\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)~\text{for any}~x,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}(x)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^{n}{\mathbb{P}}(Y_{t}\leq x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}(x)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^{n}{\mathbb{P}}(Y_{t}\leq x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the averaged CDF of observed <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the null CDF, and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^{n}F_{\bm{P}_{t}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^{n}F_{\bm{P}_{t}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the averaged alternative CDF.
As a reminder, under Model <a href="#Thmmodel2" title="Model 2 (Surrogate model). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{1},\ldots,\bm{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{1},\ldots,\bm{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed, unknown, and predetermined so that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is free of randomness.
Equation (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.3 Estimation Methods ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) implies that for any weight function <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v:{\mathbb{R}}\mapsto{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v:{\mathbb{R}}\mapsto{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it must satify</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB_{\bar{F}}[v]=(1-{\varepsilon})\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]+{\varepsilon}\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝐏</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB_{\bar{F}}[v]=(1-{\varepsilon})\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]+{\varepsilon}\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where expectations <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB_{\bar{F}}[v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB_{\bar{F}}[v]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB_{F_{0}}[v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB_{F_{0}}[v]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝐏</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v]</annotation></semantics></math> represent integrals with respect to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Rearranging this expression gives</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}=\frac{\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\bar{F}}[v]}{\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝐏</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}=\frac{\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\bar{F}}[v]}{\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Equation <a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.3 Estimation Methods ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> forms the basis for estimators inspired by moment methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, which match observed sample moments with theoretical ones. Here, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB_{F_{0}}[v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB_{F_{0}}[v]</annotation></semantics></math> is computable because <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is known or can be approximated through Monte Carlo simulation. For the observed mixture samples <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB_{\bar{F}}[v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB_{\bar{F}}[v]</annotation></semantics></math> is estimated by the sample average <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^{n}v(Y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^{n}v(Y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the term <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝐏</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v]</annotation></semantics></math> is more challenging because <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown and depends on the NTP distributions <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{1},\ldots,\bm{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{1},\ldots,\bm{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which vary across tokens. Estimating these NTP distributions directly is impractical due to their high dimensionality and variability. For example, in models like OPT-1.3B, the vocabulary size <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{{\mathcal{W}}}|=50,272" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>50,272</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{{\mathcal{W}}}|=50,272</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, meaning each <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a distribution over tens of thousands of tokens. With text lengths <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> often around 100, the number of parameters far exceeds the number of samples, making estimation infeasible.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Initial attempt by ignoring <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</h6>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To simplify, we initially ignore <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> by choosing the weight function <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v_{\mathrm{ind}}(x)={\bf 1}\{x\leq\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>ind</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\mathrm{ind}}(x)={\bf 1}\{x\leq\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a small constant <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">specified by the verifier</span>. For small <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the unknown term <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> becomes negligible compared to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> because Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmasmp1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ Identifiability for continuous pivotal statistics. ‣ 3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> requires <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\delta\to 0}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\delta\to 0}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
This leads to the following estimator:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}(\delta)=1-\frac{\widehat{F}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ini</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}(\delta)=1-\frac{\widehat{F}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{F}(x)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^{n}{\bf 1}\{Y_{t}\leq x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{F}(x)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^{n}{\bf 1}\{Y_{t}\leq x\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical CDF of the observed pivotal statistics. While this estimator is simple, it has two significant limitations. First, relying on a small <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> means only a few samples are used which reduces statistical efficiency. For example, if <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen too small, most observed values <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> may exceed <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to unstable estimates due to limited sample sizes. Second, the choice of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\mathrm{ind}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>ind</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\mathrm{ind}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is heuristic, and better weight functions may improve performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Refinement by estimating <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</h6>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome these limitations, we refine the estimator by approximating <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of assuming it is negligible. Instead of estimating each <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> individually, we estimate the averaged alternative CDF <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> using pivotal statistics collected from a comparable open-source model. Specifically, let <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\mathrm{wm}}_{1},\ldots,Y^{\mathrm{wm}}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>wm</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>wm</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\mathrm{wm}}_{1},\ldots,Y^{\mathrm{wm}}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denote pivotal statistics derived from purely watermarked texts, and define their empirical CDF as <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{t=1}^{N}{\bf 1}\{Y^{\mathrm{wm}}_{t}\leq x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>wm</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{t=1}^{N}{\bf 1}\{Y^{\mathrm{wm}}_{t}\leq x\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since this estimation process can be performed prior to observing our samples, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is treated as a non-random CDF. Incorporating this additional information, the refined estimator is given by</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)=\frac{F_{0}(\delta)-\widehat{F}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)-\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>rfn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)=\frac{F_{0}(\delta)-\widehat{F}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)-\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Optimal weight function.</h6>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we address the choice of weight function <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To gain some insights, let’s consider an ideal case where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is perfectly estimated such that <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}=\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}=\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case,
the variance of the resulting estimator</span> <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\widehat{F}}[v]}{\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝐏</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\widehat{F}}[v]}{\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v]}</annotation></semantics></math> for a given weight function <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is upper bounded by <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Proof of Inequality () ‣ Appendix A Proofs of Theoretical Analysis ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> for the proof)</span></p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}\left(\frac{\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\widehat{F}}[v]}{\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v]}\right)\leq\frac{\mathrm{Var}_{\bar{F}}(v)}{n(\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v])^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝐏</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Var</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝐏</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}\left(\frac{\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\widehat{F}}[v]}{\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v]}\right)\leq\frac{\mathrm{Var}_{\bar{F}}(v)}{n(\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v])^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">One can verify that (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Optimal weight function. ‣ 3.3 Estimation Methods ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) reduces to an exact equation when <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are i.i.d. from <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under Model <a href="#Thmmodel2" title="Model 2 (Surrogate model). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, where each <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}\sim(1-{\varepsilon})F_{0}+{\varepsilon}F_{\bm{P}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}\sim(1-{\varepsilon})F_{0}+{\varepsilon}F_{\bm{P}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, this condition holds if the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are also i.i.d. generated. Our minimax lower bound is established under this setting.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then minimize this variance upper bound to determine the optimal weight function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmlem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmlem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmlem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Optimal weight function)<span id="S3.Thmlem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmlem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmlem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmlem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Inequality <a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Optimal weight function. ‣ 3.3 Estimation Methods ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, the optimal value over all weight functions satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{v}\frac{\mathrm{Var}_{\bar{F}}(v)}{[\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v]]^{2}}=\left[\int\frac{[1-g(x)]^{2}}{(1-{\varepsilon})+{\varepsilon}g(x)}{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)\right]^{-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>v</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Var</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝐏</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{v}\frac{\mathrm{Var}_{\bar{F}}(v)}{[\EB_{{F}_{0}}[v]-\EB_{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}}[v]]^{2}}=\left[\int\frac{[1-g(x)]^{2}}{(1-{\varepsilon})+{\varepsilon}g(x)}{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)\right]^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmlem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmlem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the optimal solution (up to constant factors) is</span></p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\mathrm{opt}}(x)=\frac{1-g(x)}{(1-{\varepsilon})+{\varepsilon}g(x)},~\text{with}~g(x)=\frac{{\mathrm{d}}\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)}{{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>opt</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">with</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\mathrm{opt}}(x)=\frac{1-g(x)}{(1-{\varepsilon})+{\varepsilon}g(x)},~\text{with}~g(x)=\frac{{\mathrm{d}}\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)}{{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, directly using this optimal weight function <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\mathrm{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\mathrm{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> presents a challenge: it depends on both <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are unknown. While <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be estimated empirically by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, substituting <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\mathrm{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\mathrm{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> into (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.3 Estimation Methods ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) introduces <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> into both sides of the equation, creating a self-referential problem. To resolve this, we define the refined estimator <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the fixed point of the following operator, that is, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}=\widehat{{\mathcal{T}}}({\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}=\widehat{{\mathcal{T}}}({\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}})</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{{\mathcal{T}}}({\varepsilon})={\mathcal{P}}\left(\frac{\int\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}({\varepsilon},x)\left[{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)-{\mathrm{d}}\widehat{F}(x)\right]}{\int\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}({\varepsilon},x)\left[{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)-{\mathrm{d}}\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)\right]}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{{\mathcal{T}}}({\varepsilon})={\mathcal{P}}\left(\frac{\int\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}({\varepsilon},x)\left[{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)-{\mathrm{d}}\widehat{F}(x)\right]}{\int\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}({\varepsilon},x)\left[{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)-{\mathrm{d}}\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)\right]}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> projects <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> onto the interval <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\varepsilon}_{\min},1-{\varepsilon}_{\min}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\varepsilon}_{\min},1-{\varepsilon}_{\min}]</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure numerical stability. Here, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sufficiently small number (e.g., <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>) <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">to ensure numerical stability</span> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical version of the optimal weight function, defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}({\varepsilon},x)=\frac{1-\widehat{g}(x)}{(1-{\varepsilon})+{\varepsilon}\widehat{g}(x)},\quad\widehat{g}(x)=\frac{{\mathrm{d}}\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)}{{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}({\varepsilon},x)=\frac{1-\widehat{g}(x)}{(1-{\varepsilon})+{\varepsilon}\widehat{g}(x)},\quad\widehat{g}(x)=\frac{{\mathrm{d}}\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)}{{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{g}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{g}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> represents an empirical estimate of the density ratio function <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{g}(x)=\frac{{\mathrm{d}}\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)}{{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{g}(x)=\frac{{\mathrm{d}}\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(x)}{{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math>, with its specific form detailed in the experiments section. Since <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{g}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{g}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed prior to the proportion estimation, we treat it as a non-random function in the theoretical analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The intuition behind <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is that it balances two key factors: the weight function <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> leverages information about <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> to improve accuracy, while the fixed-point formulation ensures that the unknown <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is consistently estimated. This approach effectively incorporates both theoretical insights and empirical data to refine the estimation process.
As Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmlem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ Optimal weight function. ‣ 3.3 Estimation Methods ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> implies, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}</annotation></semantics></math> generally exists and is unique because the operator is a contraction mapping.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmlem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmlem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmlem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmlem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmlem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmlem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that <math id="S3.Thmlem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;D_{\mathrm{TV}}(F_{0},\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">TV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐏</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;D_{\mathrm{TV}}(F_{0},\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.Thmlem5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, <math id="S3.Thmlem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{x}|\widehat{g}(x)|&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">sup</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{x}|\widehat{g}(x)|&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmlem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB_{\bar{F}}[\widehat{g}]^{-2}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB_{\bar{F}}[\widehat{g}]^{-2}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span>.
If <math id="S3.Thmlem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{x}\frac{|\widehat{g}(x)-g(x)|}{\min\{1,\widehat{g}(x)\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{x}\frac{|\widehat{g}(x)-g(x)|}{\min\{1,\widehat{g}(x)\}}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small and <math id="S3.Thmlem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large, then with high probability, <math id="S3.Thmlem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{{\mathcal{T}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{{\mathcal{T}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a contraction mapping on the interval <math id="S3.Thmlem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\varepsilon}_{\min},1-{\varepsilon}_{\min}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\varepsilon}_{\min},1-{\varepsilon}_{\min}]</annotation></semantics></math> for small values of <math id="S3.Thmlem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Theoretical Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present a theoretical analysis of the estimators introduced earlier. Formal theories and detailed proofs for all results are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proofs of Theoretical Analysis ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Accuracy for Initial Attempts</h4>

<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> be fixed.
Under Model <a href="#Thmmodel2" title="Model 2 (Surrogate model). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, it follows that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB\left|{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}(\delta)-{\varepsilon}+{\varepsilon}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)}\right|\leq\frac{\sigma_{n}}{\sqrt{n}}~~\text{where}~~\sigma_{n}^{2}=\frac{\bar{F}(\delta)(1-\bar{F}(\delta))}{[F_{0}(\delta)]^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ini</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝐏</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">where</mtext><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB\left|{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}(\delta)-{\varepsilon}+{\varepsilon}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)}\right|\leq\frac{\sigma_{n}}{\sqrt{n}}~~\text{where}~~\sigma_{n}^{2}=\frac{\bar{F}(\delta)(1-\bar{F}(\delta))}{[F_{0}(\delta)]^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmcor2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcor2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2</span></span><span id="Thmcor2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcor2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcor2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcor2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Following Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4.1 Accuracy for Initial Attempts ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB\left|{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}(\delta_{n})-{\varepsilon}\right|\leq\frac{1}{\sqrt{nF_{0}(\delta_{n})}}\cdot\sqrt{(1-{\varepsilon})+{\varepsilon}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta_{n})}{F_{0}(\delta_{n})}}+{\varepsilon}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta_{n})}{F_{0}(\delta_{n})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ini</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>nF</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝐏</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝐏</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB\left|{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}(\delta_{n})-{\varepsilon}\right|\leq\frac{1}{\sqrt{nF_{0}(\delta_{n})}}\cdot\sqrt{(1-{\varepsilon})+{\varepsilon}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta_{n})}{F_{0}(\delta_{n})}}+{\varepsilon}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta_{n})}{F_{0}(\delta_{n})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcor2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcor2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmasmp1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ Identifiability for continuous pivotal statistics. ‣ 3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, the right hand side <math id="Thmcor2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmcor2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{n}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{n}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcor2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nF_{0}(\delta_{n})\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nF_{0}(\delta_{n})\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="Thmcor2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4.1 Accuracy for Initial Attempts ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> shows that the mean absolute error (MAE) of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ini</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB|{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}(\delta)-{\varepsilon}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ini</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB|{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}(\delta)-{\varepsilon}|</annotation></semantics></math>, is of the order <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\sigma_{n}}{\sqrt{n}}+\text{bias}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mtext>bias</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\sigma_{n}}{\sqrt{n}}+\text{bias}</annotation></semantics></math>. The noise term, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\sigma_{n}}{\sqrt{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\sigma_{n}}{\sqrt{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, arises from the randomness in the collected data.
The bias term, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)}</annotation></semantics></math>, is due to neglecting the unknown term <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the estimator <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ini</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In general, this bias cannot be eliminated, as we will show in Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Inherent Bias Caused by Non-identifiability ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> using the green-red list watermark as an example.
However, for watermarks satisfying Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmasmp1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ Identifiability for continuous pivotal statistics. ‣ 3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, this bias can be mitigated by setting <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently small, leveraging the condition <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim\limits_{\delta\to 0}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim\limits_{\delta\to 0}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, as show in Corollary <a href="#Thmcor2" title="Corollary 2. ‣ 4.1 Accuracy for Initial Attempts ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
In summary, adaptively tuning <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> can reduce the bias and make the estimator asymptotically unbiased.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Accuracy for Refinements</h4>

<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{g}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{g}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o(1)</annotation></semantics></math>) denote a quantity that converges to zero when <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{x}\frac{|\widehat{g}(x)-g(x)|}{\min\{1,\widehat{g}(x)\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{x}\frac{|\widehat{g}(x)-g(x)|}{\min\{1,\widehat{g}(x)\}}</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n}</annotation></semantics></math>) converges to zero.
Under Model <a href="#Thmmodel2" title="Model 2 (Surrogate model). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, it follows that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB|{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)-{\varepsilon}|\leq\frac{{\sigma}_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}+o_{g}(1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>rfn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB|{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)-{\varepsilon}|\leq\frac{{\sigma}_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}+o_{g}(1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;D_{\mathrm{TV}}(F_{0},\bar{F}_{\bm{P}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">TV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐏</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;D_{\mathrm{TV}}(F_{0},\bar{F}_{\bm{P}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup\limits_{x}|{g}(x)|&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi></munder><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup\limits_{x}|{g}(x)|&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB_{F_{0}}[g]^{-2}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">F</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB_{F_{0}}[g]^{-2}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, then either setting <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> as a small constant or choosing <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}_{\min}=\frac{\log n}{\sqrt{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">min</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}_{\min}=\frac{\log n}{\sqrt{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\EB|{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}-{\varepsilon}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\EB|{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}-{\varepsilon}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\lesssim\frac{{\tau}_{n}^{\star}+o(1)}{\sqrt{n}}+o_{g}(1)+{\varepsilon}_{\min}{\bf 1}\{{\varepsilon}\notin[{\varepsilon}_{\min},1-{\varepsilon}_{\min}]\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≲</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lesssim\frac{{\tau}_{n}^{\star}+o(1)}{\sqrt{n}}+o_{g}(1)+{\varepsilon}_{\min}{\bf 1}\{{\varepsilon}\notin[{\varepsilon}_{\min},1-{\varepsilon}_{\min}]\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{n}^{\star}\geq\tau_{n}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{n}^{\star}\geq\tau_{n}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> are two variance terms defined by</span></p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma_{n}^{\star}]^{2}=\frac{\bar{F}(\delta)(1-\bar{F}(\delta))}{[F_{0}(\delta)-\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)]^{2}}\quad\text{and}\quad[\tau_{n}^{\star}]^{2}=\left[\int\frac{[1-g(x)]^{2}}{(1-{\varepsilon})+{\varepsilon}g(x)}{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)\right]^{-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma_{n}^{\star}]^{2}=\frac{\bar{F}(\delta)(1-\bar{F}(\delta))}{[F_{0}(\delta)-\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)]^{2}}\quad\text{and}\quad[\tau_{n}^{\star}]^{2}=\left[\int\frac{[1-g(x)]^{2}}{(1-{\varepsilon})+{\varepsilon}g(x)}{\mathrm{d}}F_{0}(x)\right]^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The error bound (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4.2 Accuracy for Refinements ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) remains valid when <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\EB_{F_{0}}[g]^{-2}=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">F</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\EB_{F_{0}}[g]^{-2}=\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}_{\min}=\frac{\log n}{n^{1/4}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">min</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}_{\min}=\frac{\log n}{n^{1/4}}</annotation></semantics></math>, but achieving it requires a stronger condition: <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{1/4}\cdot\sup_{x}\frac{|\widehat{g}(x)-g(x)|}{\min{1,\widehat{g}(x)}}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.055em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi></msub><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{1/4}\cdot\sup_{x}\frac{|\widehat{g}(x)-g(x)|}{\min{1,\widehat{g}(x)}}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS6.SSS2" title="A.6.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ A.6 Analysis for the Optimal Estimator ‣ Appendix A Proofs of Theoretical Analysis ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6.2</span></a>.<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4.2 Accuracy for Refinements ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> provides an analysis of the MAEs for the refined estimators <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>rfn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The analysis assumes that the density ratio estimate <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently accurate, satisfying the condition <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{x}\frac{|\widehat{g}(x)-g(x)|}{\min\{1,\widehat{g}(x)\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{x}\frac{|\widehat{g}(x)-g(x)|}{\min\{1,\widehat{g}(x)\}}</annotation></semantics></math> being small.
For <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>rfn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, the MAE is given by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\sigma_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\sigma_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, analyzing the MAE for <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}</annotation></semantics></math> requires additional moment conditions, which are necessary to study the fixed-point solutions of the operator <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{{\mathcal{T}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{{\mathcal{T}}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Optimal weight function. ‣ 3.3 Estimation Methods ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). Under these conditions, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves an MAE of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\tau_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\tau_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, if we ignore higher-order terms and assume the stability constant <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as a valid lower bound for the true proportion <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\tau_{n}^{\star}]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\tau_{n}^{\star}]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the optimal value of the minimization problem in Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmlem4" title="Lemma 3.4 (Optimal weight function). ‣ Optimal weight function. ‣ 3.3 Estimation Methods ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, as a result of which, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{n}^{\star}\geq\tau_{n}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{n}^{\star}\geq\tau_{n}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. This guarantees that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently achieves a smaller MAE than <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>rfn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, highlighting the advantage of using the optimal weight function.
As will be shown in the experiments, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}</annotation></semantics></math> not only achieves better accuracy but also exhibits higher stability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Minimax Lower Bounds</h4>

<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a given <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be independently generated under Model <a href="#Thmmodel2" title="Model 2 (Surrogate model). ‣ 3.1 Target Estimand ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the true proportion, and the NTP distribution is uniformly sampled from the set <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{P}_{1},\ldots,\bm{P}_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{P}_{1},\ldots,\bm{P}_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}({\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}({\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> denote a small open interval containing a given proportion <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma_{n}^{\star}]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma_{n}^{\star}]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\tau_{n}^{\star}]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\tau_{n}^{\star}]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the variance term defined in (<a href="#S4.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4.2 Accuracy for Refinements ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I1.i1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the set of all measurable functions of the indicators </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{1}\{Y_{1}\leq\delta\},\ldots,\mathbf{1}\{Y_{n}\leq\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\{Y_{1}\leq\delta\},\ldots,\mathbf{1}\{Y_{n}\leq\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}\in{\mathcal{G}}}\sup_{{\varepsilon}_{0}\in{\mathcal{B}}({\varepsilon})}\EB|\widehat{{\varepsilon}}-{\varepsilon}_{0}|\geq\frac{\sigma_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{2n}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}\in{\mathcal{G}}}\sup_{{\varepsilon}_{0}\in{\mathcal{B}}({\varepsilon})}\EB|\widehat{{\varepsilon}}-{\varepsilon}_{0}|\geq\frac{\sigma_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{2n}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I1.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the set of all measurable functions of the random vector </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}\in{\mathcal{G}}}\sup_{{\varepsilon}_{0}\in{\mathcal{B}}({\varepsilon})}\EB|\widehat{{\varepsilon}}-{\varepsilon}_{0}|\geq\frac{\tau_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{2n}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}\in{\mathcal{G}}}\sup_{{\varepsilon}_{0}\in{\mathcal{B}}({\varepsilon})}\EB|\widehat{{\varepsilon}}-{\varepsilon}_{0}|\geq\frac{\tau_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{2n}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4. ‣ 4.3 Minimax Lower Bounds ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> establishes minimax lower bounds for MAEs under Model 2, which characterizes the best achievable accuracy in a worst-case scenario.
The worst case coincides with the setting where the <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>’s are drawn i.i.d. from a large pool. While this may appear simplified, it reflects a realistic constraint: real-world verifiers lack access to exact NTP distributions and must rely on aggregate statistics to refine estimation.
In our formulation, these bounds (either <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\sigma_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⋆</mo></msubsup><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\sigma_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\tau_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">τ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⋆</mo></msubsup><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\tau_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>) are expressed in terms of the averaged CDF <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This CDF <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> captures key distributional properties of the alternative distributions without requiring knowledge of individual <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>’s, and it can be reasonably estimated using similar LLMs on related tasks.
In contrast, more general bounds would require knowing which token corresponds to which <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is practically infeasible.
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The implications are twofold. First, these lower bounds are tight and achieved by our estimators, up to the estimation error <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{g}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{g}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> in estimating <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">rfn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> matches the bound <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\sigma_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⋆</mo></msubsup><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\sigma_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> among indicator-based estimators, while <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}</annotation></semantics></math> attains the smaller bound <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\tau_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">τ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⋆</mo></msubsup><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\tau_{n}^{\star}}{\sqrt{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> among all measurable estimators. Second, achieving these bounds critically depends on incorporating the correction term <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>; without it, the initial estimator fails to reach the optimal rate, as detailed in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Analysis for the Initial Estimator ‣ Appendix A Proofs of Theoretical Analysis ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Inherent Bias Caused by Non-identifiability</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The green-red list watermark does not satisfy Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmasmp1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ Identifiability for continuous pivotal statistics. ‣ 3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, so we cannot expect the aforementioned estimators to obtain high accuracy for this watermark. One piece of evidence is that, although Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4.1 Accuracy for Initial Attempts ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> still holds for the green-red list watermark, the bias term <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)}={\varepsilon}\left(1-{\varepsilon}+{\varepsilon}\frac{1-\bar{\mu}_{t}}{1-\gamma}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}\frac{\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}(\delta)}{F_{0}(\delta)}={\varepsilon}\left(1-{\varepsilon}+{\varepsilon}\frac{1-\bar{\mu}_{t}}{1-\gamma}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and will never vanish as <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is consistent with the non-identifiability issue: <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mu}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mu}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is unobservable since each <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is unobservable (see Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ A general framework for watermarking. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for their relation), and the binary <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> signals provide negligible information about <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mu}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mu}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Even attempting to estimate <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mu}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mu}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> fails to resolve this issue. For instance, applying MLE to the i.i.d. binary mixture model in Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmlem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ Impossibility for the green-red list watermark. ‣ 3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> to estimate unknown parameters <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> introduces inherent bias, as shown in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4.4 Inherent Bias Caused by Non-identifiability ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}\overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim}(1-{\varepsilon})\text{Ber}(\gamma)+{\varepsilon}\text{Ber}(\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">i.i.d.</mtext></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Ber</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Ber</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}\overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim}(1-{\varepsilon})\text{Ber}(\gamma)+{\varepsilon}\text{Ber}(\mu)</annotation></semantics></math>, where only <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&gt;\mu&gt;\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&gt;\mu&gt;\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) are unknown. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{e}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the average of these <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> samples. Consider MLE that minimizes the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-regularized negative log-likelihood loss:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\lambda},\widehat{\mu}_{\lambda})=\arg\min\left\{-\log L({\varepsilon},\mu)+\lambda(\mu^{2}+{\varepsilon}^{2})\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\lambda},\widehat{\mu}_{\lambda})=\arg\min\left\{-\log L({\varepsilon},\mu)+\lambda(\mu^{2}+{\varepsilon}^{2})\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L({\varepsilon},\mu)=[(1-{\varepsilon})\gamma+{\varepsilon}\mu]^{\widehat{e}}\left[1-(1-{\varepsilon})\gamma-{\varepsilon}\mu\right]^{1-\widehat{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L({\varepsilon},\mu)=[(1-{\varepsilon})\gamma+{\varepsilon}\mu]^{\widehat{e}}\left[1-(1-{\varepsilon})\gamma-{\varepsilon}\mu\right]^{1-\widehat{e}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then have the following results</span></p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\lambda\to 0}\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\lambda}=x\sqrt{x^{2}+\gamma},\quad\lim_{\lambda\to 0}\widehat{\mu}_{\lambda}=x^{2}+\gamma," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.889em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\lambda\to 0}\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\lambda}=x\sqrt{x^{2}+\gamma},\quad\lim_{\lambda\to 0}\widehat{\mu}_{\lambda}=x^{2}+\gamma,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is the solution to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{3}\sqrt{x^{2}+\gamma}=\widehat{e}-\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{3}\sqrt{x^{2}+\gamma}=\widehat{e}-\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As we will see in Figure <a href="#S5.F1.sf5" title="Figure 1(e) ‣ Figure 1 ‣ How we obtain 𝑔̂. ‣ 5.1 Simulation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(e)</span></a>, the estimate <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{{\varepsilon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{{\varepsilon}}</annotation></semantics></math> almost never equals the ground truth <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> even when <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large and <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is very small. This case is very different from standard linear regression, where regularized MLE
(almost surely) converges to the underlying true parameter as regularization diminishes and sample size diverges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. This discrepancy arises because non-identifiability is an inherent problem of the (watermarking) model, not a flaw of the estimation methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h3>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first evaluate estimation accuracy and inherent bias on simulation datasets and then check performance on open-source LLMs. Additional details and results are in the appendix. The code is available at: <a href="https://github.com/lx10077/WatermarkProportion" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/lx10077/WatermarkProportion</a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Simulation Study</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, we use a vocabulary of size <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{{\mathcal{W}}}|=10^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{{\mathcal{W}}}|=10^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and generate 2,500 sequences of 400 tokens each, yielding <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> pivotal statistics as watermarked samples. The generation process mimics LLM behavior with a control parameter <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. For each token <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct its distribution <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by uniformly sampling the largest probability from <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{U}(0,1-\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{U}(0,1-\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math> and interpolating between Zipf’s law <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> and uniform distributions to ensure valid probabilities over <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathcal{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathcal{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Failure on green-red list watermarks. ‣ B.1 Further Details ‣ Appendix B Simulation Study: Details and Results ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). We then sample <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}\sim\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}\sim\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To simulate mixed-source data, we generate <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> null samples from <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Across 200 uniformly spaced values <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, we create mixture datasets combining <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(1-{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(1-{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> null samples with <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> watermarked samples to estimate each <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>. We aggregate all watermarked statistics into a single dataset for estimating <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, though some pivotal statistics in this aggregated dataset may be entirely irrelevant to the current task of estimating a specific <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">An equivalence for implementation.</h6>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is known, the probability integral transform <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> ensures that <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}(Y_{1}),\ldots,F_{0}(Y_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}(Y_{1}),\ldots,F_{0}(Y_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> are i.i.d. <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> samples. We can therefore assume without loss of generality that <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}={U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}={U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1,\bm{P}}=F_{\bm{P}}\circ F_{0}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑷</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1,\bm{P}}=F_{\bm{P}}\circ F_{0}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the generalized inverse. Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmasmp1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ Identifiability for continuous pivotal statistics. ‣ 3.2 Estimability ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> remains valid under this transformation.
For all experiments, we apply this transform to ensure <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}={U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}={U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, so our pivotal statistics are basically <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}(Y_{1}),\ldots,F_{0}(Y_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}(Y_{1}),\ldots,F_{0}(Y_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. For simplicity, we continue denoting them as <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1},\ldots,Y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">How we obtain <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</h6>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The approach to estimating the density ratio <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is highly flexible, with many methods available in the statistical literature.
We estimate the density ratio <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> using a simple histogram-based approach. The data in <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is divided into <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=500</annotation></semantics></math> bins, with bin heights normalized to form probability densities that integrate to 1, yielding a piecewise constant estimator. While more sophisticated methods like kernel density estimation could provide smoother estimates, they are computationally prohibitive for the large datasets (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math>).
The histogram approach offers better computational efficiency and scalability.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F1" class="ltx_figure">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Figure <a href="#S5.F1.sf1" title="Figure 1(a) ‣ Figure 1 ‣ How we obtain 𝑔̂. ‣ 5.1 Simulation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a> shows the CDFs of mixture sample distributions for the Gumbel-max watermark with <math id="S5.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and varying <math id="S5.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figures <a href="#S5.F1.sf2" title="Figure 1(b) ‣ Figure 1 ‣ How we obtain 𝑔̂. ‣ 5.1 Simulation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>, <a href="#S5.F1.sf3" title="Figure 1(c) ‣ Figure 1 ‣ How we obtain 𝑔̂. ‣ 5.1 Simulation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(c)</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F1.sf4" title="Figure 1(d) ‣ Figure 1 ‣ How we obtain 𝑔̂. ‣ 5.1 Simulation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(d)</span></a> show MAEs of three estimators across various ground truth <math id="S5.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> values when <math id="S5.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figure <a href="#S5.F1.sf5" title="Figure 1(e) ‣ Figure 1 ‣ How we obtain 𝑔̂. ‣ 5.1 Simulation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(e)</span></a> shows the MLE results for estimating both <math id="S5.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> in the case of i.i.d. non-identifiable mixture binary data.
</span></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22343v1/gumbel_Delta0.1+_cdf.svg" id="S5.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CDFs of mixture dist.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22343v1/finalfig-Gumbel-K1000N2500c5key2846513T400Delta0.1+-IterFalse.svg" id="S5.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">MAEs for Gumbel-max</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
