</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">CDFs of mixture distributions.</h6>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F1.sf1" title="Figure 1(a) ‣ Figure 1 ‣ How we obtain 𝑔̂. ‣ 5.1 Simulation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a> shows the CDFs of mixture sample distributions for the Gumbel-max watermark when <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the CDFs diverge further from the uniform distribution, which corresponds to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}=0</annotation></semantics></math> (pure null samples). When <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the mixture consists entirely of watermarked samples, resulting in the greatest deviation from uniformity.
Additional results for other values of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and the inverse transform watermark are provided in Figures <a href="#A2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ B.2 Empirical CDFs of Mixture Distributions ‣ Appendix B Simulation Study: Details and Results ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ B.2 Empirical CDFs of Mixture Distributions ‣ Appendix B Simulation Study: Details and Results ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> in the appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Estimation accuracy.</h6>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now examine the estimation accuracy, measured by the mean absolute errors (MAEs) on the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> mixed-source samples. Three estimators are compared: (i) <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">INI</span>, the initial estimator <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ini</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{ini}}}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> tuned with the best <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{10^{-1},10^{-2},10^{-3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{10^{-1},10^{-2},10^{-3}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, (ii) <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">IND</span>, the refined estimator <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>rfn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{rfn}}}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> similarly tuned, and (iii) <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">OPT</span>, the refined estimator <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\mathrm{opt}}}</annotation></semantics></math> employing the optimal weight function <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Optimal weight function. ‣ 3.3 Estimation Methods ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figures <a href="#S5.F1.sf2" title="Figure 1(b) ‣ Figure 1 ‣ How we obtain 𝑔̂. ‣ 5.1 Simulation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F1.sf3" title="Figure 1(c) ‣ Figure 1 ‣ How we obtain 𝑔̂. ‣ 5.1 Simulation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(c)</span></a> visualize the MAEs of these estimators at various ground truth values <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The results reveal that <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">OPT</span> consistently outperforms both <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">INI</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">IND</span> across all <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> values, particularly at higher <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, where watermarked samples dominate. This shows the benefits of the optimal weight function <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>opt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{v}_{\mathrm{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> in improving estimation accuracy. While less accurate than <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">OPT</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">IND</span> still shows improvements over <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">INI</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Averaged MAEs calculated over 200 ground truth <math id="S5.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> values which are uniformly distributed on <math id="S5.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Standard deviations are provided in parentheses, and all values are reported in units of <math id="S5.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Bold numbers denote the best performance.
</span></figcaption>
