</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As further evidence, Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Estimation accuracy. ‣ 5.1 Simulation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents the averaged MAEs across different <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> values. For both watermark types, the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">OPT</span> estimator consistently achieves the lowest average MAEs with the smallest standard deviations, as highlighted by the bold numbers. This shows not only the accuracy of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">OPT</span> but also its stability across varying conditions.
For example, in the Gumbel-max watermark setting with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">OPT</span> records an MAE of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, a significant improvement over <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">INI</span> (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="210\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>210</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">210\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>) and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">IND</span> (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>23</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>).
This performance pattern persists across other <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> values, showing the consistent superiority of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">OPT</span>.
The improvement of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">OPT</span> over <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">IND</span> further implies the critical role of the optimal weight function in enhancing estimation accuracy. This is expected, as the weight function is derived by minimizing the variance of the estimator (see Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmlem4" title="Lemma 3.4 (Optimal weight function). ‣ Optimal weight function. ‣ 3.3 Estimation Methods ‣ 3 Proportion Estimation Methods ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>), thus inherently providing robustness over diverse settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Non-identifiability.</h6>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Our estimators are expected to fail on the green-red list watermark because <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is not identifiable in this case, given that NTP distributions <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{P}_{1:n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{1:n}</annotation></semantics></math> are inaccessible.
A further issue is that binary <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y^{\mathrm{wm}}_{1:n}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">wm</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≡</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\mathrm{wm}}_{1:n}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math>, which results in <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≡</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math> and provides no information about <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to abnormal estimation behavior. Figure <a href="#S5.F1.sf4" title="Figure 1(d) ‣ Figure 1 ‣ How we obtain 𝑔̂. ‣ 5.1 Simulation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(d)</span></a> confirms this theoretical prediction: <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">INI</span> often gives zero estimates while <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">IND</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">OPT</span> give estimates near one.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inherent bias.</h6>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We then empirically validate the inherent bias predicted by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4.4 Inherent Bias Caused by Non-identifiability ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> by generating <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. samples from <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-{\varepsilon})\text{Ber}(\gamma)+{\varepsilon}\text{Ber}(\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Ber</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Ber</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-{\varepsilon})\text{Ber}(\gamma)+{\varepsilon}\text{Ber}(\mu)</annotation></semantics></math>, computing the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-regularized MLE via (<a href="#S4.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4.4 Inherent Bias Caused by Non-identifiability ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), and plotting <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> against <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S5.F1.sf5" title="Figure 1(e) ‣ Figure 1 ‣ How we obtain 𝑔̂. ‣ 5.1 Simulation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(e)</span></a> shows results for <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma,\mu)=(0.3,0.9)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma,\mu)=(0.3,0.9)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently deviates from true <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming the predicted bias. The red dashed line representing the theoretical limit from (<a href="#S4.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4.4 Inherent Bias Caused by Non-identifiability ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) closely matches empirical estimates. Similar patterns across different parameter settings (Figure <a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ B.3 Additional Results on Estimation Accuracy ‣ Appendix B Simulation Study: Details and Results ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> in the appendix) show that non-identifiability renders standard estimation methods ineffective.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Open-source Model Experiments</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We evaluate watermark proportion estimation using open-source LLMs under a controlled setup. We begin by sampling 500 documents from two corpora: the news-like C4 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> and the scientific arXiv dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. For each document, we extract the last 50 tokens as a prompt and generate 500 continuation tokens using one of three models: OPT-1.3B, OPT-13B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, or LLaMA3.1-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
To introduce mixture data, at each step <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the LLM generates <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> using a specified watermarking scheme with probability <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>; with the remaining probability, it samples <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> directly from <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑷</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> without watermarking.
We further apply 1-sequence repeated context masking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, which watermarks <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> only if the current context window <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{w}_{(t-m):(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}_{(t-m):(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is unique in the generation history. This technique improves text quality but lowers the realized watermark proportion below the nominal <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding a more realistic mix of watermarked and unwatermarked tokens.
All experiments are conducted under two temperature settings <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{T}\in\{0.7,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.7</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{T}\in\{0.7,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We adopt <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">WPL</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> as our baseline since no existing method directly estimates watermark proportions.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2</span></span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote2.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We exclude another token-level localization method, </span><span id="footnote2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">SeedBS</span><span id="footnote2.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="footnote2.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a><span id="footnote2.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="footnote2.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, due to its poor accuracy and prohibitive runtime.</span></span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">WPL</span> is a token-level localization method that assigns a binary label to each token indicating whether it is watermarked, and the overall proportion can be estimated by averaging these labels.
</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
MAEs of estimators across various true proportions <math id="S5.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> and temperature parameters on the OPT-1.3B model and C4 dataset. Additional results for other models and datasets are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Open-source LLM Experiments: Details and Results ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.
</span></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22343v1/whole-finalfig-1p3B-Gumbel-N0500-temp0.7-IterFalse.svg" id="S5.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Gumbel-max with <math id="S5.F2.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{T}=0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{T}=0.7</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22343v1/whole-finalfig-1p3B-Gumbel-N0500-temp1-IterFalse.svg" id="S5.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Gumbel-max with <math id="S5.F2.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{T}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{T}=1</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22343v1/whole-finalfig-1p3B-Inverse-N0500-temp0.7-IterFalse.svg" id="S5.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S5.F2.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Inverse with <math id="S5.F2.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{T}=0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{T}=0.7</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22343v1/whole-finalfig-1p3B-Inverse-N0500-temp1-IterFalse.svg" id="S5.F2.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="S5.F2.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Inverse with <math id="S5.F2.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{T}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{T}=1</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Estimation accuracy.</h6>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MAE results for the 1.3B-OPT model and C4 dataset are shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.2 Open-source Model Experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, while results for other LLMs and datasets, with similar performance patterns, are provided in the appendix. Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Estimation accuracy. ‣ 5.2 Open-source Model Experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> summarizes the mean and standard deviation of MAEs, offering an overview of estimation performance across models, temperatures, and watermarks. In nearly all cases, the optimal estimator <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">OPT</span> achieves the smallest error with the least variation, demonstrating strong empirical performance. In contrast, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">WPL</span> performs much worse, with MAEs higher than any of our estimators. This result is expected, as token-level localization is inherently more challenging than estimating a global proportion, making <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">WPL</span> suboptimal for our task.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
