<figure id="A2.F7.sf7" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22343v1/GreenRed-gamma0.5-mu0.7.svg" id="A2.F7.sf7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F7.sf7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(g)</span> </span><math id="A2.F7.sf7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma,\mu)=(0.5,0.7)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">γ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">μ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.5</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.7</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma,\mu)=(0.5,0.7)</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A2.F7.sf8" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22343v1/GreenRed-gamma0.5-mu0.9.svg" id="A2.F7.sf8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F7.sf8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(h)</span> </span><math id="A2.F7.sf8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma,\mu)=(0.5,0.9)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">γ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">μ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.5</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.9</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma,\mu)=(0.5,0.9)</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A2.F7.sf9" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22343v1/GreenRed-gamma0.7-mu0.9.svg" id="A2.F7.sf9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F7.sf9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(i)</span> </span><math id="A2.F7.sf9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma,\mu)=(0.7,0.9)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">γ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">μ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.7</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.9</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma,\mu)=(0.7,0.9)</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="A2.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">More results illustrating the inherent bias caused by non-identifiability. We vary the values of <math id="A2.F7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math>. The red curves represent the limit proportion from Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4.4 Inherent Bias Caused by Non-identifiability ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, while the other colored curves show the MLE estimates for different sample sizes <math id="A2.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Additional Results on Inherent Bias</h4>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We vary the values of <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> and plot the estimators obtained by solving the <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-regularized MLEs described in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4.4 Inherent Bias Caused by Non-identifiability ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> in Figure <a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ B.3 Additional Results on Estimation Accuracy ‣ Appendix B Simulation Study: Details and Results ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. In each subplot, we fix the <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> regularization coefficient to <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> and consider three sample sizes: <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{10^{3},10^{4},10^{5}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{10^{3},10^{4},10^{5}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The red curves represent the theoretical limit proportion <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{{\varepsilon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{{\varepsilon}}</annotation></semantics></math> from Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4.4 Inherent Bias Caused by Non-identifiability ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, while the other colored curves depict the MLE estimates <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{{\varepsilon}}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> for the different sample sizes. Across all combinations of <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-regularized MLEs align closely with the theoretical limit proportion. This alignment validates our theory and provides strong support for the existence of inherent bias.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Open-source LLM Experiments: Details and Results</h3>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Details on Mixture Data Setting</h4>

<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Watermark generation.</h6>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use a context window of size <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=4</annotation></semantics></math>, where the random variable <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is derived as <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\zeta_{t}={\mathcal{A}}(s_{(t-m):(t-1)},{\mathtt{Key}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝙺𝚎𝚢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{t}={\mathcal{A}}(s_{(t-m):(t-1)},{\mathtt{Key}})</annotation></semantics></math>, based on the preceding <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. The randomness is generated using the hash function <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>.
To avoid repetitive outputs, a watermark is applied only when the current text window is unique within the generation history, following the strategies outlined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. This technique is known as 1-sequence repeated context masking in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. If no watermark is applied, tokens are sampled directly from the NTP distribution using multinomial sampling.
When constructing a dataset with the true proportion <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, we aim for approximately <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>-proportion of the samples (i.e., observed pivotal statistics) to be watermarked and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>-proportion to be null samples.
So we independently watermark each token with probability <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, applying 1-sequence repeated context masking can reduce the effective watermark proportion, as it may prevent some tokens that should be watermarked from being marked as such.
As a result, in our experiments, we calculate the average number of watermarked tokens to determine the ground truth <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Prompt generation.</h6>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our prompt generation approach largely follows the methodology described in Appendix C.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Prompts are created by sampling documents from the news-focused section of the C4 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. Each document is required to have at least 50 tokens, and shorter documents are excluded. Since retokenization may not perfectly align with the original token counts, we ensure that the verifier always processes at least <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> tokens by adding special padding tokens, which are model-dependent. Additionally, to reduce the need for padding, we generate extra tokens—referred to as buffer tokens—beyond <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. In all experiments, 20 buffer tokens are generated, which generally eliminates the necessity for padding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Details for <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">IND</span> and <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">OPT</span>.</h6>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The computation for the refined estimators (namely <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">IND</span> and <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">OPT</span>) follows the procedure outlined in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Simulation Study: Details and Results ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. To evaluate MAEs across various proportions <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate a mixed-source dataset for each trial, ensuring that approximately <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>-proportion of the samples are watermarked. Importantly, these datasets consist solely of pivotal statistics, not tokens.
After generating datasets for different values of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, we aggregate all the watermarked pivotal statistics into a fully watermarked dataset. This combined dataset is then used to estimate <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>𝑷</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{F}_{\bm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>. As a result, some pivotal statistics are entirely relevant to the current estimation task.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Details for the baseline <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">WPL</span>.</h6>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">WPL</span>, proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. [44]</a></cite>, uses an existing online learning algorithm to compute a watermark score for each token, classifying it as watermarked if the score exceeds a specified threshold. For the Gumbel-max watermark, the score is defined as <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\log(1-Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\log(1-Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, while for the inverse transform watermark, it is simply <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding pivotal statistics <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are summarized in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ A general framework for watermarking. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. To ensure a fair and competitive comparison, we tune the threshold over a predefined set and report the best result that achieves the lowest MAE across both figures and tables. Specifically, the threshold set is <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1.3,1.5,1.7\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.7</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1.3,1.5,1.7\}</annotation></semantics></math> for Gumbel-max and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0.73,0.75,0.77\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0.73</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.75</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.77</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0.73,0.75,0.77\}</annotation></semantics></math> for the inverse transform watermark.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Additional Results on Estimation Accuracy</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figures <a href="#A3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ C.2 Additional Results on Estimation Accuracy ‣ Appendix C Open-source LLM Experiments: Details and Results ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#A3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ C.2 Additional Results on Estimation Accuracy ‣ Appendix C Open-source LLM Experiments: Details and Results ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> illustrate the estimation performance of various estimators on the OPT-13B and LLaMA3.1-8B models using the C4 dataset, respectively.
Figure <a href="#A3.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ C.2 Additional Results on Estimation Accuracy ‣ Appendix C Open-source LLM Experiments: Details and Results ‣ Appendices ‣ Optimal Estimation of Watermark Proportions in Hybrid AI-Human Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> presents the performance on the arXiv dataset.
In most scenarios, <span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">OPT</span> achieves the lowest MAE, while <span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">WPL</span> exhibits poor accuracy when the true proportion is around 0.5.</p>
</div>
